### 2021MNRAS.503.4320F: ALMA-IRDC - II. First high-angular resolution measurements of the <SUP>14</SUP>N/<SUP>15</SUP>N ratio in a large sample of infrared-dark cloud cores (2021; star_formation, astrochemistry, milky_way_ism; observational_alma_primary)
- question: What is the $^{14}$N/$^{15}$N ratio in N$_{2}$H$^{+}$ at high angular resolution across dense cores in infrared-dark clouds, and how does it relate to surrounding diffuse gas and core physical or evolutionary properties?
- step 2: Identify 3 mm continuum cores and N2H+ intensity peak cores using 2D dendrograms
  - method: Apply 2D dendrogram analysis to 3 mm continuum maps (from Paper I) and to 2D integrated intensity maps of N2H+ (1-0) [Results: "focus the $^{14}$N/$^{15}$N analysis first on the 3 mm continuum sources identified in each IRDC using the 2D dendrogram method (Rosolowsky et al. (cite: rosolowsky08))."] [Results: "These have been identified here by conducting the dendrogram analysis on the (2D) integrated intensity maps of N$_{2}$H$^{+}$ (see paper I)."]
  - parameter: For N2H+ dendrogram: min_value = 30 sigma ~ 4.5 K km/s, min_delta = 30 sigma, min_pix = 3 beam ~ 150 pixels [Results: "The following set of parameters are used for determination of the dendrogram structure: min_value = 30 $\sigma$ $\sim$ 4.5 K kms (the minimum intensity considered in the analysis); min_delta = 30 $\sigma$(the minimum spacing between isocontours); min_pix = 3 beam $\sim$ 150 pixels (the minimum number of pixels contain…"]
  - validation: Choice of input parameters in the continuum dendrogram analysis does not significantly affect core identification as demonstrated in Paper I [Results: "We point out that the choice of the input parameters in the dendrogram analysis does not significantly affect the identification of the cores as demonstrated in paper I."]
