  - method: Convert clean cubes to FITS format, extract spectra over continuum core contours, N2H+ core contours, and total power beam positions using GILDAS MAPPING, smooth to ~0.4 km/s resolution, and convert integrated flux density F_nu to brightne… [Observations and data reduction: "Clean cubes extracted around the lines of interest were then converted into fits format and further analysed with packages of the gildas software. In particular, the spectra of N$_{2}$H$^{+}$ and N$^{15}$NH$^+$(1–0) were extracted with mapping, and analysed in the way described in Sect. 3 with class."] [3.1.1 Extraction of the spectra and lin…: "The integrated flux density, $F_{\nu}$, of each spectrum has been converted to brightness temperature units via the formula: $$\begin{equation} T_{\rm B}(K) \simeq 1222 F_{\nu}{\rm (mJy)}/[ \nu^2{\rm (GHz)}\Theta^2 (\prime \prime)] \;, \label{eq:conversion} \end{equation}$$"]
  - software: GILDAS, MAPPING, CLASS
  - parameter: Spectral smoothing to ~0.4 km s^-1; Total power HPBW ~ 70" [Results: "The N$_{2}$H$^{+}$(1–0) line was clearly detected with excellent signal to noise ($\geq 20$) in all clouds (see Figs. 8 – 11) after smoothing to a uniform spectral resolution of $\sim 0.4$ km s$^{-1}$."] [4.1 $^{14}$N/$^{15}$N ratios in the con…: "The half power beam width of the total power data is $\sim 70$$^{\prime\prime}$ for both N$^{15}$NH$^+$ and N$_{2}$H$^{+}$ (1–0), so that the spectra extracted from one position can contain the contribution of nearby cores."]
  - assumption: Eq. 1 is valid for non-spherical sources using equivalent circle angular diameter Theta [3.1.1 Extraction of the spectra and lin…: "We stress that Eq.([\[eq:conversion\]](#eq:conversion)) is valid also for non-spherical sources as long as $\Theta$ is the source equivalent angular diameter."]
  - validation: Checked consistency of detections between continuum and N2H+ cores and examined noise levels in H3-n2hp-2 vs H3c3 [3.2 Spectra towards the N$_{2}$H$^{+}$ …: "Furthermore, and most importantly, the noise in the H3-n2hp-2 spectrum is about 5 times higher than that in the H3c3 one (see Fig. 3), and the hint of a possible line under the noise level is apparent in H3-n2hp-2 as well."]
- step 4: Fit N15NH+ (1-0) and N2H+ (1-0) spectral lines and integrate hyperfine components
  - method: Fit N15NH+ (1-0) with a single Gaussian to the main F=2-1 component (or simultaneous hyperfine fit using CLASS method hfs in D5c5/6/7, F1c3, F4c5, F4c7); evaluate N2H+ (1-0) isolated F1=0-1 component by comparing peak velocities with N15NH… [3.1.2 Line fits: N$^{15}$NH$^+$ (1–0): "Therefore, the main components have been fitted with a single Gaussian, except in the four cores mentioned above, for which we have fitted simultaneously all components using the CLASS software (method hfs in CLASS):"] [3.1.4 Derivation of the total column de…: "Therefore, we have estimated the N$_{2}$H$^{+}$ total column density, $N_{\rm tot}$(N$_{2}$H$^{+}$), from the integrated intensity of the hyperfine component F$_1 = 0-1$, which is well separated in velocity from the others and intrinsically the faintest one"]
  - software: GILDAS, CLASS
  - parameter: Relative strength of N2H+ F1=0-1 component is 1/9 of total (multiply by 9); relative strength of N15NH+ F=2-1 component is 0.555 (divide by 0.555) [3.1.4 Derivation of the total column de…: "This was then converted to the total integrated intensity of the line by multiplying it by 9, which is the relative strength of the sum of all components with respect to the F$_1 = 0-1$ one (see Table 1)."] [3.1.4 Derivation of the total column de…: "for the spectra in which the main hyperfine component only is detected, i.e. the F$=2-1$ one (see Table 1), we have estimated the total integrated intensity by dividing the integral of this component for its relative strength (i.e. $\sim 0.555$, Table 1)."]
  - assumption: Velocity separation of hyperfine components fixed to laboratory values; all components have the same T_ex and FWHM [3.1.2 Line fits: N$^{15}$NH$^+$ (1–0): "the method considers that the velocity separation of the components is fixed to the laboratory values, and that all components have the same excitation temperature ($T_{\rm ex}$) and the same line full width at half maximum (FWHM)."]
