### 2024A&A...691A.121N: A 260 pc resolution ALMA map of HCN(1─0) in the galaxy NGC 4321 (2024; nearby_galaxies, star_formation; observational_alma_primary)
- question: How do the dense gas fraction (HCN/CO) and dense gas star formation efficiency (SFR/HCN) vary across morphological environments and ISM pressure at 260 pc resolution over the full disc of NGC 4321, and is there a pressure threshold for den…
- step 1: Reduce and image the ALMA HCN data
  - method: Data reduced with the PHANGS-ALMA pipeline (CASA-based), producing an HCN PPV cube combining 12-m, ACA 7-m and TP data [2.2 New ALMA maps of HCN: "The data reduction was performed using the PHANGS–ALMA pipeline (details can be found in (cite: Leroy2021a))"]
  - software: PHANGS-ALMA pipeline, CASA
  - parameter: ~8 mK noise per 5 km/s channel [2.2 New ALMA maps of HCN: "The resulting HCN position-position-velocity cube has $\sim\SI{8}{\milli\kelvin}$ noise per 5 km s^−1^ channel."]
  - parameter: Channel width 5 km/s used in the analysis [3.1 Integrated intensity maps: "is the channel width of 5 km s^−1^"]
- step 2: Homogenise CO and ancillary maps to the HCN beam and grid
  - method: Convolve CO to HCN resolution; resample all data onto a hexagonal beam-spaced spaxel grid and common spectral axis; convolve 2D maps (Halpha, 21 um) to 260 pc and reproject [3.1 Integrated intensity maps: "First, we homogenise the data by convolving the CO data to the HCN resolution"] [3.1 Integrated intensity maps: "we convolve the maps to the 260 HCN resolution and reproject them onto the same beam-size hexagonal pixel grid"]
  - software: astropy
  - parameter: Hexagonal grid with beam-size spaxel separation [3.1 Integrated intensity maps: "Then, we adopt a hexagonal spaxel grid with a beam-size spaxel separation and sample all data to the same spaxel grid and spectral axis."]
  - assumption: Every hexagonal pixel is an independent line of sight [3.1 Integrated intensity maps: "This means that every hexagonal pixel is an independent line-of-sight (LOS) measurement."]
- step 3: Build velocity masks and integrated intensities
  - method: CO-based pixel-by-pixel velocity masks applied to CO and HCN cubes, summing T x channel width over masked channels; uncertainty from emission-free channel rms times sqrt(N_mask) [3.1 Integrated intensity maps: "Then, we create velocity masks based on the CO on a pixel-by-pixel basis to select the velocity range where we also expect to find HCN emission."] [3.1 Integrated intensity maps: "where $\sigma_{T_{\rm line}}$ is the standard deviation in the emission-free channels (i.e. channels not selected by the mask)"]
  - parameter: 4 sigma mask expanded into channels above 2 sigma [3.1 Integrated intensity maps: "This is done by building a $4\sigma$ mask that is expanded into channels above $2\sigma$ in order to recover broader emission belonging to a $4\sigma$ core"]
  - parameter: Detection defined as S/N >= 3 per independent sight line [Table notes (stacked intensities): "Shown are the number of detected ($\rm{S/N}\geq 3$) independent sight lines"]
  - assumption: HCN emission lies in the same velocity range as CO [3.1 Integrated intensity maps: "to select the velocity range where we also expect to find HCN emission"]
- step 4: Derive molecular gas surface density, HCN/CO and f_dense
  - method: Sigma_mol from CO(2-1) with alpha_CO and R21, cos(i) inclination correction; HCN/CO as proxy of f_dense (f_dense ~ 2.1 W_HCN/W_CO(2-1)); two cases: constant conversion factors, and spatially varying alpha_CO and R21 (Bolatto et al. 2013 eq… [3.2 Molecular gas surface density – CO: "using constant $\alpha_{\text{CO}}$ and $R_{21}$ conversion factors"] [3.2 Molecular gas surface density – CO: "using spatially varying $\alpha_{\text{CO}}$ and $R_{21}$"]
  - parameter: alpha_CO = 4.35 Msun pc^-2 (K km/s)^-1 (constant case) [3.3 Dense gas fraction – HCN/CO: "$\ensuremath{\alpha_{\text{CO}}}=\SI{4.35}{\Msun\per\square\parsec}\,(\SI{}{\Kkms})^{-1}$ (cite: Bolatto2013)"]
  - parameter: alpha_HCN = 14 Msun pc^-2 (K km/s)^-1 [3.3 Dense gas fraction – HCN/CO: "$\ensuremath{\alpha_{\text{HCN}}}=\SI{14}{\Msun\per\square\parsec}\,(\SI{}{\Kkms})^{-1}$ (cite: Onus2018)"]
  - parameter: R21 = 0.65 (constant); varying case uses a 4 arcsec R21 map from archival CO(1-0), median 0.54, scatter 0.16 [3.3 Dense gas fraction – HCN/CO: "a fiducial $\text{CO(2--1)}$-to-$\text{CO(1--0)}$ line ratio of $R_{21}=0.65$ (cite: denBrok2022; Leroy2022)"] [7.1.2 Spatially varying conversion fact…: "Here, we measure a median $R_{21}=0.54$ and a scatter of 0.16."]
  - parameter: Metallicity from MUSE via Gaussian Process Regression; fiducial GMC surface density fixed at 100 Msun/pc^2; iterative solution [7.1.2 Spatially varying conversion fact…: "applying a Gaussian Process Regression (GPR) to model the 2D metallicity distribution (cite: Williams2022)"] [7.1.2 Spatially varying conversion fact…: "we adopt a fiducial GMC surface density of 100"]
  - assumption: Constant alpha_HCN, so f_dense is proportional to HCN/CO [3.2 Molecular gas surface density – CO: "We use a constant $\alpha_{\text{CO}}$ for the HCN-to-CO line ratio due to the poor knowledge about variations of the HCN-to-dense gas conversion factor thus keeping $f_{\text{dense}}$ proportional to HCN/CO."]
  - validation: Compared with multi-line alpha_CO of Teng et al. 2023 in the central 2 kpc [7.1.2 Spatially varying conversion fact…: "are on average 0.20 smaller than the values used here, but consistent at the 1-sigma level within 0.21"]
- step 5: Derive SFR surface density and SFR/HCN
  - method: Balmer-decrement corrected Halpha converted to SFR with the Calzetti et al. 2007 factor; SFE_dense proxy is SFR/HCN; alternative 21 um and 33 GHz tracers used to check Halpha [3.4 Star formation rate (SFR) – Halpha: "We use Balmer decrement-corrected $\text{H}\alpha$ maps to measure the star formation rate surface density"] [3.5 Dense gas star formation efficiency…: "We take the ratio of the star formation rate surface density"]
  - parameter: C_Halpha = 5.3e-42 (Calzetti et al. 2007); extinction curve of O'Donnell 1994 [3.4 Star formation rate (SFR) – Halpha: "using the conversion factor $C_{\ensuremath{\text{H}\alpha}}=\num{5.3e-42}$ from (cite: Calzetti2007)"] [3.4 Star formation rate (SFR) – Halpha: "Those rely on the extinction curve from (cite: O’Donnell1994) as described in (cite: Pessa2022) and (cite: Belfiore2023)."]
  - assumption: Constant star formation history, age 100 Myr, solar metallicity, Kroupa IMF [3.4 Star formation rate (SFR) – Halpha: "This conversion assumes a constant star formation history, age of 100 , solar metallicity, and a (cite: Kroupa2001) initial mass function (IMF)"]
  - validation: Halpha SFR agrees with 33 GHz SFR within 20 percent and with a linear 21 um SFR within a factor 0.2 in the centre [9.3 SFR tracer comparison: "agreeing within 20 %."] [3.4 Star formation rate (SFR) – Halpha: "21 $\mu$ m emission also yields similar SFR values (within 0.2) when adopting a linear conversion"]
- step 6: Assign morphological environments
  - method: Use the Querejeta et al. 2021 'simple' mask from S4G 3.6 um; bar ends defined as overlap of spiral arms with the bar; interbar and interarm combined; resampled onto the HCN hexagonal grid [3.7 Morphological environmental masks: "We use the “simple” mask, where each pixel is uniquely assigned to a dominant environment."] [3.7 Morphological environmental masks: "For simplicity, we combine interbar, interarm into one region, referred to as interarm."]
- step 9: Fit piecewise-linear relations of HCN/CO and SFR/HCN with radius and pressure
  - method: MARS (R package earth) fit to the binned data with at most two linear segments to find thresholds; LinMix fit to individual sight lines for slope uncertainties [3.8 Stacking and linear regression: "model to the binned data in order to find the best piece-wise linear regression function that describes the data"] [3.8 Stacking and linear regression: "Here, we force the model to only consist of up to two linear functions, i.e. it can either find one or two regimes"]
  - software: earth (R), LinMix
  - parameter: New split point accepted only if chi^2 improves by more than 0.01 [3.8 Stacking and linear regression: "meaning that the $\chi^2$ value is improved by more than 0.01"]
  - assumption: Binned stacks recover the underlying relation without population bias from non-detections [3.8 Stacking and linear regression: "We fit the stacked data in order to probe the underlying global relation without “population” biases and to not be dominated by non-detections in constraining the best-fit line."]
  - fitting: MARS minimises chi^2 over split points (priors not applicable); LinMix is a Bayesian regression run via MCMC (priors, chain settings not stated); MARS gives no slope errors so LinMix sight-line fits provide them [Footnote 6: "is a Bayesian inference tool to linear regression, which can take into account upper limits and infers the posterior distribution of the fit line parameters via MCMC simulation"] [3.8 Stacking and linear regression: "We use these sight-line fits to quantify the uncertainty of the regression slopes since the MARS fitting routine (see below) does not yield uncertainties."]
  - validation: LinMix fits to individual sight lines agree within 1 sigma with the binned fits [3.8 Stacking and linear regression: "resulting in similar fit relations in agreement within 1-sigma uncertainties with the fits reported here for the binned data"]
- step 10: Fit HCN/CO(1-0) versus Sigma_mol at 260 pc
  - method: Stack and fit HCN/CO(1-0) (CO(2-1) converted with the R21 map) against beam-matched Sigma_mol using the varying alpha_CO; compare with literature relations and a constant alpha_CO case [4.5 HCN/CO as a density tracer: "we stack and fit the data to obtain the mean relation"] [4.5 HCN/CO as a density tracer: "using a constant $\alpha_{\text{CO}}$ yields a shallower relation (slope of 0.48) compared to 0.61 with a varying"]
  - software: earth (R), LinMix
  - parameter: Data below Sigma_mol = 10 Msun/pc^2 excluded from the fit [4.5 HCN/CO as a density tracer: "we exclude data below $\ensuremath{\Sigma_{\text{mol}}}<\SI{10}{\Msun\per\square\parsec}$"]
  - fitting: Same stacking plus MARS/LinMix approach as step 9 [4.5 HCN/CO as a density tracer: "we stack and fit the data to obtain the mean relation"]
  - validation: Fits excluding the centre still show positive slopes of 0.25 (centre) and 0.35 (disc) [4.5 HCN/CO as a density tracer: "slopes of 0.25 (centre) and 0.35 (disc)"]
- result: HCN cube noise = ~8 ± not stated mK per 5 km/s channel [2.2 New ALMA maps of HCN: "The resulting HCN position-position-velocity cube has $\sim\SI{8}{\milli\kelvin}$ noise per 5 km s^−1^ channel."]
- result: Independent lines of sight with HCN emission in the map = 302 ± not stated count [2.2 New ALMA maps of HCN: "yielding detection of 302 independent lines of sight in HCN emission"]
- result: Detected HCN sight lines (S/N >= 3), full galaxy = 275 ± not stated count [4.1 Dense gas spectroscopic ratios: "allows analysis of 275 detected sight lines"]
- result: Detected HCN sight lines by environment = centre 42, bar 70, bar ends 26, spiral arms 97, interarm 40 ± not stated count [4.1 Dense gas spectroscopic ratios: "we detect 42 HCN sight lines in the centre, 70 in the bar, 26 in the bar ends, 97 in the spiral arms, and 40 in the interarm regions"]
- result: Mean HCN/CO of detections in the centre = 0.089 ± 0.0003 dimensionless [4.1.1 HCN/CO variations: "reaches values around $0.1$ (mean of $0.089\pm 0.0003$)"]
- result: Mean SFR/HCN in the centre = 0.025 ± 0.0001 (Msun/yr/kpc^2)/(K km/s) [4.1.2 SFR/HCN variations: "SFR/HCN decreases towards the centre of the galaxy (mean of $0.025\pm 0.0001$"]
- result: Mean SFR/HCN in the bar = 0.039 ± 0.001 (Msun/yr/kpc^2)/(K km/s) [4.1.2 SFR/HCN variations: "we find SFR/HCN to be particularly low in the bar of the galaxy (mean of $0.039\pm 0.001$)"]
- result: Mean SFR/HCN in bar ends, spiral arms and interarm (range) = 0.086 to 0.274 ± 0.003 to 0.057 (Msun/yr/kpc^2)/(K km/s) [4.1.2 SFR/HCN variations: "have means between $0.086\pm 0.003$ and $0.274\pm 0.057$"]
- result: HCN/CO versus r_gal slope, r_gal <= 2.0 kpc = -0.38 ± 0.02 dex per kpc (slope as stated, units not given) [4.2.1 HCN/CO vs galactocentric radius: "we measure a very strong (slope $m=-0.38\pm 0.02$, Pearson correlation coefficient $\rho=-0.86$"]
- result: HCN/CO versus r_gal slope in the disc (r_gal > 2.0 kpc) = 0.0 ± 0.01 as stated (slope) [4.2.1 HCN/CO vs galactocentric radius: "HCN/CO remains constant on average ($m=0.0\pm 0.01$"]
- result: SFR/HCN versus r_gal slope, r_gal <= 2.8 kpc = 0.34 ± 0.02 as stated (slope) [4.2.2 SFR/HCN vs galactocentric radius: "SFR/HCN varies systematically with radius ($m=0.34\pm 0.02$"]
- result: MARS radial break radii = 2.0 kpc (HCN/CO), 2.8 kpc (SFR/HCN) ± not stated kpc [4.2 Radial trends: "The MARS model finds two regimes in each of the radial correlations, which separates the relations into a central region ($\leq\SI{2.0}{\kilo\parsec}$ for HCN/CO and $\leq\SI{2.8}{\kilo\parsec}$ SFR/HCN)"]
- result: HCN/CO versus <P_DE> slopes, high and low pressure = high 0.42, low 0.18 ± 0.03 each as stated (slope) [4.3.1 HCN/CO vs pressure: "The correlation is steeper in the high-pressure ($m=0.42\pm 0.03$) regime compared to the low-pressure regime ($m=0.18\pm 0.03$)."]
- result: HCN/CO versus <P_DE> single-line slope = 0.35 ± 0.02 as stated (slope) [4.3.1 HCN/CO vs pressure: "However, the realtion could also be well fitted with a single linear function with $m=0.35\pm 0.02$."]
- result: SFR/HCN versus <P_DE> slopes, high and low pressure = high -0.89, low -0.32 ± high 0.22, low 0.04 as stated (slope) [4.3.2 SFR/HCN vs pressure: "($\rho=-0.39$, $p=0.003$,$m=-0.89\pm 0.22$)"]
- result: SFR/HCN pressure threshold = 1.6e6 ± not stated kB K cm^-3 [5.1 Pressure threshold for dense gas an…: "with the threshold being at $\ensuremath{\langle P_{\text{DE}}\rangle}_{\rm threshold}=\SI{1.6e6}{\kB\kelvin\per\cubic\centi\metre}$"]
- result: Pressure thresholds found by MARS in the HCN/CO and SFR/HCN relations = ~4e5 (HCN/CO) and ~1e6 (SFR/HCN) ± not stated kB K cm^-3 [4.3 ISM pressure relations: "We find a pressure threshold in both relations (HCN/CO and SFR/HCN) at $\ensuremath{\langle P_{\text{DE}}\rangle}\approx\SI{4e5}{\kB\kelvin\per\cubic\centi\metre}$ and $\ensuremath{\langle P_{\text{DE}}\rangle}\approx\SI{1e6}{\kB\kelvin\per\cubic\centi\metre}$"]
- result: Correlation of average HCN/CO with <P_DE> = 0.75 ± not stated dimensionless (Pearson rho) [4.3.1 HCN/CO vs pressure: "suggesting that the ISM pressure is well correlated with the average HCN/CO ($\rho=0.75$) over almost three orders of magnitude in pressure"]
- result: Bar SFR/HCN offset below other environments at fixed pressure = ~0.5 ± not stated dex [5.3 Star formation suppression in the b…: "the average trend of the bar is $\approx\SI{0.5}{\dex}$ lower compared to the other environments"]
- result: log10(HCN/CO(1-0)) vs log10(Sigma_mol) fit: slope, intercept, scatter = slope 0.61, intercept -2.64, scatter 0.28 ± slope 0.10, intercept 0.21 dimensionless (slope, intercept in dex); scatter in dex [Table 4: "0.61 (0.10) | -2.64 (0.21) | 0.28 | 260 | 260 | beam-matched | this work"]
- result: HCN/CO vs Sigma_mol slope with constant alpha_CO = 0.48 ± not stated dimensionless [4.5 HCN/CO as a density tracer: "using a constant $\alpha_{\text{CO}}$ yields a shallower relation (slope of 0.48) compared to 0.61 with a varying"]
- result: Scatter of 260 pc HCN/CO vs Sigma_mol relation compared with kpc scale = 0.28 versus 0.14 ± not stated dex [4.5 HCN/CO as a density tracer: "the scatter in the individual 260 sight-line measurements is twice as large (0.28) as the scatter at kpc-scales (0.14)"]
- result: Median R21 and scatter = 0.54 ± scatter 0.16 dimensionless [7.1.2 Spatially varying conversion fact…: "Here, we measure a median $R_{21}=0.54$ and a scatter of 0.16."]
- result: Median spatially varying alpha_CO = 3.3 ± scatter 0.10 as stated Msun pc^-2 (K km/s)^-1 [7.1.2 Spatially varying conversion fact…: "The median $\alpha_{\text{CO}}$ value is $\SI{3.3}{\Msun\per\square\parsec}\,(\SI{}{\Kkms})^{-1}$"]
- limitation (authors): f_dense estimates are uncertain by a factor of a few because of conversion factors, especially alpha_HCN [3.3 Dense gas fraction – HCN/CO: "such that our $f_{\text{dense}}$ estimates are expected to be uncertain by a factor of a few"]
- limitation (authors): S/N clipping biases detected-sight-line means towards high HCN/CO and low SFR/HCN [4.1.1 HCN/CO variations: "Since S/N clipping systematically selects luminous HCN regions, these values will be biased towards high HCN/CO and low SFR/HCN"]
- limitation (authors): Quoted uncertainties exclude calibration and systematic errors [Table notes (HCN/CO and SFR/HCN statist…: "do not include any calibration or systematic uncertainties"]
- not stated (card): CASA version and PHANGS-ALMA pipeline version/commit used for this project; Imaging parameters of the HCN cube (tclean weighting/robust, taper, deconvolver, cell size, masks, clean threshold, restoring beam) are not given; only the pipeline is cited; Line-free channels and any continuum subtraction used for the HCN cube; How the 12-m, 7-m and total-power data were combined (feather/joint deconvol…

### 2023ApJ...950...75I: Dense Molecular Gas Properties of the Central Kiloparsec of Nearby Ultraluminous Infrared Galaxies Constrained by ALMA Three Transition-line Observations (2023; nearby_galaxies, agn_and_quasars, astrochemistry; observational_alma_primary)
- question: What are the physical and chemical properties (H2 volume density, kinetic temperature, and HCN-to-HCO+ abundance ratio) of nuclear dense molecular gas in the central 1–2 kpc of nearby (U)LIRGs with and without luminous AGN signatures?
- step 2: Extract nuclear spectra, measure continuum flux densities, and fit Gaussian line profiles to derive line fluxes and ratios
  - method: Extract 1–2 kpc beam spectra and continuum peak fluxes at the continuum peak position; fit single or double Gaussian profiles to molecular emission lines to derive velocity-integrated fluxes and line ratios [Results: "Gaussian fits are applied to estimate the emission line fluxes within the adopted beam sizes."] [Table 9: "Cols.(4)–(7): Gaussian fit of emission line in a 1–2 kpc beam spectrum at the continuum peak position."]
  - parameter: Single Gaussian fit for all lines except HCN and HCO+ in IRAS 12112+0305 NE (double Gaussian fit) [Results: "Single Gaussian fits are adopted, except for the HCN and HCO$^{+}$ lines of IRAS 12112$+$0305 NE, which clearly exhibit a double-peaked profile with a strong central dip (Figure 1j,k) wherein the results of a double Gaussian fit are adopted."]
  - assumption: Cosmological parameters H_0 = 71 km s^-1 Mpc^-1, Omega_M = 0.27, Omega_Lambda = 0.73 [Introduction: "Throughout this study, we adopt the cosmological parameters H$_{0}$ $=$ 71 km s$^{-1}$ Mpc$^{-1}$, $\Omega_{\rm M}$ = 0.27, and $\Omega_{\rm \Lambda}$ = 0.73."]
  - fitting: Gaussian profile fitting (velocity, peak flux, FWHM) with statistical uncertainty; 3-sigma upper limit from 1.5 kpc beam moment 0 map for undetected HCN J=4-3 in IRAS 12112+0305 SW [Table 3: "Only the statistical uncertainty of the Gaussian fit is included."] [Table 3: "$^{A}$ This 3$\sigma$ upper limit is estimated from a 1.5 kpc beam moment 0 map."]
  - validation: Confirmed that flux estimates between single and double Gaussian fits agree within ~10% for significantly detected lines (>=3 sigma) [Results: "we decide to adopt single Gaussian fitting results after confirming that the flux estimates between the single and double Gaussian fits agree within $\sim$10% for significantly detected emission lines ($\gtrsim$3$\sigma$)."]
- step 3: Compare observed HCN-to-HCO+ flux ratios with RADEX non-LTE model grids
  - method: Calculate non-LTE emission line flux ratios using RADEX via pyradex over a grid of H2 density and kinetic temperature and compare in 3D ratio space (J=2-1, J=3-2, J=4-3) [General Descriptions of Our RADEX Non-L…: "To investigate nuclear (1–2 kpc) molecular gas properties in detail, the observed emission line flux ratios are compared with the calculated flux ratios using RADEX (cite: RADEX)."] [General Descriptions of Our RADEX Non-L…: "For implementation, `pyradex`, a Python wrapper for RADEX, is used."]
  - software: RADEX, pyradex
  - parameter: n_H2 = 10^3–10^8 cm^-3, T_kin = 10–1000 K, fiducial N_HCO+ = 1 x 10^16 cm^-2, [HCN]/[HCO+] = 1, 3, and 7 [HCN-to-HCO+ Emission Line Flux Ratios: "In the RADEX model calculations for the (U)LIRGs’ nuclei, we set density and temperature in the range 10$^{3}$–10$^{8}$ cm$^{-3}$ and 10–1000 K, respectively."] [HCN-to-HCO+ Emission Line Flux Ratios: "We also adopt the HCO$^{+}$ column density with N$_{\rm HCO+}$ = 1 $\times$ 10$^{16}$ cm$^{-2}$ as the fiducial value"]
  - assumption: Static and spherically symmetric homogeneous medium, background temperature = 2.73 K, H2 as only collision partner, HNC excluded [General Descriptions of Our RADEX Non-L…: "In all RADEX non-LTE calculations, molecular gas is assumed to be static and spherically symmetric homogeneous medium. The background temperature is set as the cosmic microwave background (2.73 K). The collision partner for HCN, HCO$^+$, and HNC is regarded as H$_2$ only."] [General Descriptions of Our RADEX Non-L…: "To perform the RADEX non-LTE calculations, we use only the HCN and HCO$^{+}$ data, but exclude the HNC data for the following two reasons."]
  - fitting: Visual comparison in 3D flux ratio space [HCN-to-HCO+ Emission Line Flux Ratios: "Figure 3 shows a three-dimensional plot of the HCN-to-HCO$^+$ emission line flux ratio at J=2–1, J=3–2, and J=4–3."]
  - validation: Tested sensitivity to lower column density (N_HCO+ = 1 x 10^15 cm^-2) and broader line width (Delta v = 800 km s^-1) in Appendix D [HCN-to-HCO+ Emission Line Flux Ratios: "We also perform calculations using the HCO$^{+}$ column density of N$_{\rm HCO+}$ = 1 $\times$ 10$^{15}$ cm$^{-2}$ (an order of magnitude smaller than that adopted in Figure 3) and a line width of $\Delta$v = 800 km s$^{-1}$ (a factor of $>$2 broader)."]
- step 4: Constrain H2 gas density and kinetic temperature via least-squares fitting of high-J to low-J line ratios
  - method: Fit four observed high-J to low-J flux ratios (J=3-2/J=2-1 and J=4-3/J=2-1 of HCN and HCO+) with RADEX models using Levenberg-Marquardt algorithm in lmfit, optionally preceded by L-BFGS-B optimization of flux calibration scaling factors [High-J to Low-J Emission Line Flux Rati…: "To quantitatively estimate the density and temperature of molecular gas at (U)LIRGs’ nuclei, a total of four observed line flux ratios, J=3–2 to J=2–1 and J=4–3 to J=2–1 of HCN and HCO$^+$ (Table 5) are fitted using the RADEX model."] [High-J to Low-J Emission Line Flux Rati…: "Least-squares fitting of log n$_{\rm H_2}$ (density) and log T$_{\rm kin}$ (temperature) is performed using conventional Levenberg-Marquardt algorithm through Python package `lmfit` (cite: lmfit)."]
  - software: RADEX, pyradex, lmfit, SciPy
  - parameter: Grid of log n_H2 from 2 to 6 and log T_kin from 1 to 3; fiducial N_HCO+ = 1 x 10^16 cm^-2 and [HCN]/[HCO+] = 3; line widths Delta v from Table 3 (200 to 800 km s^-1) [High-J to Low-J Emission Line Flux Rati…: "The confidence intervals for the parameters are examined by grid computing $\Delta\chi^2\equiv\chi^2-\chi^2_{\rm best}$ with log n$_{\rm H_2}$ ranging from 2–6 and log T$_{\rm kin}$ from 1–3."] [High-J to Low-J Emission Line Flux Rati…: "Thus, we perform the same fit for the remaining ULIRGs by adopting the fiducial values of \[HCN\]/\[HCO$^{+}$\] = 3 and N$_{\rm HCO^+}$ = 1 $\times$ 10$^{16}$ cm$^{-2}$."]
  - assumption: Absolute flux calibration uncertainty scaling up to 5% for Band 5 and 10% for Bands 6 and 7 (doubled for Cycle 0) [High-J to Low-J Emission Line Flux Rati…: "The absolute flux calibration uncertainty is as high as 5% for J21a (band 5) and 10% for J32a and J43a (bands 6 and 7), respectively, according to the ALMA Proposer’s Guide. However, for Cycle 0 observations, we conservatively choose to adopt twice the above values"]
  - fitting: Levenberg-Marquardt least-squares minimization and Delta chi^2 grid evaluation; L-BFGS-B with box constraints when flux scaling is turned on [High-J to Low-J Emission Line Flux Rati…: "Least-squares fitting of log n$_{\rm H_2}$ (density) and log T$_{\rm kin}$ (temperature) is performed using conventional Levenberg-Marquardt algorithm through Python package `lmfit` (cite: lmfit)."] [High-J to Low-J Emission Line Flux Rati…: "Least-squares fitting with box constraints is performed with L-BFGS-B algorithm (cite: L-BFGS-B)."]
  - validation: Tested sensitivity on NGC 1614 by varying [HCN]/[HCO+] to 1 and N_HCO+ to 1 x 10^15 cm^-2 and comparing with/without flux scaling [High-J to Low-J Emission Line Flux Rati…: "Thus, the same least-squares fit is conducted by adopting (1) \[HCN\]/\[HCO$^{+}$\] = 1 (Figure 4b), and (2) N$_{\rm HCO^+}$ = 1 $\times$ 10$^{15}$ cm$^{-2}$, in addition to \[HCN\]/\[HCO$^{+}$\] = 1 (Figure 4c)."]
- step 5: Perform Bayesian MCMC analysis with all model and scaling parameters free for six bright sources
  - method: Simultaneously fit five independent emission line flux ratios using RADEX and MCMC sampling via emcee in lmfit with all parameters free [Bayesian Analysis of Molecular Emission…: "molecular gas parameters are estimated by simultaneously fitting HCN-to-HCO$^+$ and high-J to low-J emission line flux ratios using RADEX calculations, by making all parameters free, based on a Bayesian approach."] [Bayesian Analysis of Molecular Emission…: "Using the five emission line flux ratios, we explore the parameter space using a Markov Chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) sampler; `emcee` package (cite: emcee) is used in `lmfit`."]
  - software: RADEX, pyradex, lmfit, emcee, corner
  - parameter: 100 walkers, chain length = 100 * tau steps, burn-in = 5 * tau steps, thinning = 0.5 * tau steps, 19,000 effective samples [Bayesian Analysis of Molecular Emission…: "100 walkers are initialized to be distributed around that guess. The chain is run for 100$\tau$ steps, where $\tau$ is the maximum number of integrated autocorrelation time for each parameter"] [Bayesian Analysis of Molecular Emission…: "The first 5$\tau$ steps are discarded as a “burn-in” phase. Subsequently, the chain is thinned out in 0.5$\tau$ steps to create independent samples. Hence, the effective number of sampling of the posterior probability distribution is $100\times(100-5)/0.5=19,\!000$."]
  - assumption: Flat priors with bounds: log(n_H2/cm^-3) in [2, 6], log(T_kin/K) in [1, 3], log(N_HCO+/cm^-2) in [14, 17], [HCN]/[HCO+] in [0.1, 10], J21a scaling in [0.95, 1.05], J32a scaling in [0.9, 1.1], J43a scaling in [0.9, 1.1] [Bayesian Analysis of Molecular Emission…: "Flat priors with upper and lower bounds presented in Table 7 are adopted."] [Table 7: "log(n$_{\rm H_2}$/cm$^{-3}$) | 2 | 6"]
  - fitting: MCMC sampling with emcee initialized around L-BFGS-B initial guess [Bayesian Analysis of Molecular Emission…: "The first guess is made by running the L-BFGS-B solver in $\S$5.3, with the HCO$^{+}$ column density and HCN-to-HCO$^{+}$ abundance ratio unfixed, and then 100 walkers are initialized to be distributed around that guess."]
  - validation: Monitored integrated autocorrelation time tau until N/tau > 100 and compared derived posterior medians with Levenberg-Marquardt results [Bayesian Analysis of Molecular Emission…: "Therefore, we lengthen the chain while monitoring $\tau$ and terminate the calculation when $N/\tau$ exceeds 100."] [Bayesian Analysis of Molecular Emission…: "The aim is to verify whether the Bayesian approach provides consistent results regarding gas density and temperature with those derived from the previous Levenberg-Marquardt method"]
- result: NGC 1614 HCN/HCO+ J=2-1 flux ratio = 0.56 ± ±0.04 dimensionless [Table 4: "NGC 1614 (1 kpc) | 0.56$\pm$0.04 | 0.38$\pm$0.07 | 0.31$\pm$0.05"]
- result: NGC 1614 HCN/HCO+ J=3-2 flux ratio = 0.38 ± ±0.07 dimensionless [Table 4: "NGC 1614 (1 kpc) | 0.56$\pm$0.04 | 0.38$\pm$0.07 | 0.31$\pm$0.05"]
- result: NGC 1614 HCN/HCO+ J=4-3 flux ratio = 0.31 ± ±0.05 dimensionless [Table 4: "NGC 1614 (1 kpc) | 0.56$\pm$0.04 | 0.38$\pm$0.07 | 0.31$\pm$0.05"]
- result: IRAS 06035-7102 HCN J=4-3 velocity-integrated flux = 8.8 ± ±1.8 Jy km s^-1 [Table 3: "IRAS 06035$-$7102 | 2.9$\pm$0.1 | 5.1$\pm$0.2 | 8.8$\pm$1.8 | 3.6$\pm$0.1 | 6.8$\pm$0.2 | 10$\pm$1 | 1.6$\pm$0.1 | 1.9$\pm$0.2 | 2.6$\pm$0.6 | 350"]
