### 2016Natur.537..207G: Compression and ablation of the photo-irradiated molecular cloud the Orion Bar (2016; milky_way_ism, star_formation, astrochemistry; observational_alma_primary)
- question: Is the H/H2 transition at the Orion Bar edge spatially separated from the C+/C/CO transition as static PDR models predict, and what do 1-arcsec images reveal about the cloud structure and dynamics?
- step 2: Derive physical conditions via non-LTE radiative transfer
  - method: Grid of non-local non-LTE Monte Carlo models compared to CO 3-2 peak and HCO+ 4-3 integrated intensity [Methods: "we run a grid of non-local, non-LTE excitation and radiative transfer (Monte Carlo) models."]
  - parameter: N(HCO+)=5e13, N(CO)=1e18, N_H~2e22 cm^-2 [Methods: "Radiative transfer models were run for $N$(HCO$^+$)=5$\times$10$^{13}$ cm$^{-2}$, $N$(CO)=1.0$\times$10$^{18}$ cm$^{-2}$"]
  - assumption: molecular fraction f=0.8, x_e 0–1e-4, T_dust=55 K, CMB 2.7 K [Methods: "We adopted $f$=0.8 and varied $x_e$ between 0 and 10$^{-4}$."]
  - validation: Column densities consistent with previous observations; HCO+/H13CO+ ratio indicates moderate opacity [Methods: "They are consistent with previous observations in the region"]
- step 3: Density PDF of HCO+ emission
  - method: Log-normal fit to PDF of z=ln(W/<W>) for pixels >5 sigma in compressed layers [Methods: "We obtain $z_0$=0.165 and $\sigma$=0.31 from a fit (green curve)."]
  - assumption: W(HCO+ 4-3) proportional to n_H [Methods: "This reasoning justifies the use of $W_{4-3}^{{\rm HCO^+}}$ as a proxy for $n_{\rm H}$ in the region."]
  - fitting: Gaussian in z [Emission Probability Distribution Funct…: "is very close to a log-normal distribution with $p(z)=N exp(-(z-z_0)^2/2\sigma^2)$, where $z_0$ is the peak value and $\sigma$ the standard deviation."]
- result: Mean CO 3-2 peak at δx~15'' = 164 ± ±10 K [Methods: "$T_{\rm peak}^{{ \rm CO\,3-2}}$=164$\pm$10 K and $W_{4-3}^{{\rm HCO^+}}$=69$\pm$18 K km s$^{-1}$"]
- result: Mean HCO+ 4-3 integrated intensity at δx~15'' = 69 ± ±18 K km/s [Methods: "$T_{\rm peak}^{{ \rm CO\,3-2}}$=164$\pm$10 K and $W_{4-3}^{{\rm HCO^+}}$=69$\pm$18 K km s$^{-1}$"]
- result: Density of compressed substructures = (1.0±0.5)e6 ± ±0.5e6 cm^-3 [Methods: "$T$=200-300 K and $n_{\rm H}$=(1.0$\pm$0.5)$\times$10$^6$ cm$^{-3}$"]
- result: Compression factor = 5-30 ± not stated dimensionless [Methods: "Hence, the over-dense substructures have compression factors $\sim$5-30 with respect to the ambient molecular gas."]
- result: Log-normal PDF parameters (z0, sigma) = z0=0.165, sigma=0.31 ± not stated dimensionless [Methods: "We obtain $z_0$=0.165 and $\sigma$=0.31 from a fit (green curve)."]
- result: Mass of substructures = <~0.005 ± not stated Msun [Methods: "is $\lesssim$0.005 $M_{\odot}$ (a mass per unit length of 0.3-1.0 $M_{\odot}$ pc$^{-1}$)"]
- limitation (authors): Temperature uncertain due to lack of high-J CO lines at high resolution; magnetic fields need observational confirmation [Methods: "the $T$ uncertainty is determined by the lack of higher-$J$ CO lines, observed at high-angular resolution, to better constrain $T$ from models."]
- not stated (card): CLEAN masks, thresholds and weighting; Cell and image size; Line-free channels and continuum treatment; Details of the 30m–ALMA merging (scaling factors); Rotation angle and definition of the cut geometry for δx; Grid ranges and collisional rate references for the radiative transfer models; Distance to Orion adopted explicitly

### 2018A&A...610A..77H: An ALMA study of the Orion Integral Filament. I. Evidence for narrow fibers in a massive cloud (2018; star_formation, milky_way_ism; observational_alma_primary)
- question: Are all filaments bundles of fibers? Does the massive Orion ISF contain fibers like low-mass filaments, and what are their properties?
- step 1: Calibrate and image ALMA+IRAM30m N2H+ cube
  - method: Pipeline calibration; clean with 30m model; convolve to 4.5"; feather with 30m [2 ALMA Cycle-3 observations: "both single-dish and interferometric maps were combined using the task *feathering* in order maximize the recovery of the extended emission"]
  - software: CASA
  - parameter: robust = 0.5; final beam 4.5" [2 ALMA Cycle-3 observations: "a Briggs weighting with robust parameter equal to 0.5"]
- step 2: Fit hyperfine N2H+ spectra
  - method: Subfields of 5x5 spectra; template fitted manually then automatic HFS fit; keep components with S/N>=3 [7.1 Fitting procedure: "The entire dataset is subdivided into subfields of 5$\times$5 spectra."] [7.1 Fitting procedure: "All hyperfine lines are simultaneously fitted using the HFS method assuming similar excitation conditions and widths"]
  - software: GILDAS/CLASS
  - parameter: >70 000 spectra, >25 000 components with S/N>=3 [7.1 Fitting procedure: "we analysed more than 70 000 N$_2$H$^+$ (1-0) spectra in Orion. A total of $>$ 25 000 individual components are detected with S/N $\ge$ 3."]
  - assumption: Similar excitation conditions and widths; Gaussian opacity profiles [7.1 Fitting procedure: "assuming similar excitation conditions and widths, as well as Gaussian opacity profiles for all components in the multiplet"]
  - fitting: CLASS HFS least-squares; lowest possible number of components in doubtful cases [7.1 Fitting procedure: "In case of doubt, the spectra are conservatively fitted with the lowest possible number of components."]
  - validation: Residuals inspected; <5% of fits problematic [7.1 Fitting procedure: "Estimated to affect $<$ 5% of our fits"]
- step 4: Derive non-thermal velocity dispersion
  - method: sigma_NT from FWHM minus thermal term; compare to c_s(T_K) using GAS NH3 temperatures at nearest position [3.2 Kinematic properties: "we adopt the T$_K$ value for each individual N$_2$H$^+$ component from the nearest position surveyed in NH$_3$"]
  - assumption: NH3 temperatures represent dense N2H+ gas [3.2 Kinematic properties: "these NH$_3$-derived temperatures provide good estimates for the thermal state of the dense gas detected in N$_2$H$^+$"]
- step 5: Derive column density, mass and M_lin and stability
  - method: Empirical N(H2)-I(N2H+)/T_K relation from Herschel vs single-dish; sum masses per fiber, divide by length; compare to critical mass 2 sigma_eff^2/G [3.3 Linear masses and stability: "N(H_2)\ [\mathrm{cm}^{-2}]= 67.4\times10^{21}\cdot\left(\frac{I(N_2H^+)\ [\mathrm{K}\ \mathrm{km}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}]}{T_\mathrm{K}\ [\mathrm{K}]}\right)"]
  - parameter: D=414 pc; factor ~2 uncertainty [3.3 Linear masses and stability: "uncertainties of a factor $\sim$ 2 are expected for all the above estimates"]
  - assumption: Optically thin N2H+; same correlation valid at ALMA resolution [3.3 Linear masses and stability: "our mass calculations adopt an optically thin approximation for N$_2$H$^+$ emission in the ISF"]
  - fitting: least-squares linear fit [3.3 Linear masses and stability: "We have described this relationship using a least-squares fit of all the points"]
  - validation: Agrees with NGC1333 relation; opacity checked with RADEX [3.3 Linear masses and stability: "we find an excellent correspondence between Eq. [\[eq:colden\]](#eq:colden) in Orion and the results obtained from the study of the N$_2$H$^+$ emission in the NGC 1333 region"]
- result: Mean sigma_NT/c_s (ISF components) = 0.81 ± not stated dimensionless [Table 1: "| $\left<\sigma_{NT}/\mathrm{c}_s(T_\mathrm{K})\right>$ | 0.81 | 0.91 | 0.69 |"]
- result: Median M_lin of fibers = ~19 ± factor ~2 Msun/pc [3.3 Linear masses and stability: "with a median value of $\sim~19$ M$_\ensuremath{\odot}$ pc$^{-1}$"]
- result: Dense gas mass in fibers = 288 of 295 Msun (98%) ± not stated Msun [3.1 Fibrous substructure of the ISF: "Among the total 295 M$_{\ensuremath{\odot}}$ of dense gas detected in our high-S/N N$_2$H$^+$ spectra, 288 M$_{\ensuremath{\odot}}$ (i.e. 98%) are recovered as fibers"]
- result: N2H+ to N(H2) conversion slope = 67.4e21 ± factor 2-3 cm^-2 per (K km/s / K) [3.3 Linear masses and stability: "N(H_2)\ [\mathrm{cm}^{-2}]= 67.4\times10^{21}\cdot"]
- result: Fraction of components subsonic = 76.4% ± not stated percent [Table 1: "$\sigma_{NT}/\mathrm{c}_s(T_\mathrm{K})\le$ 1 | 76.4% | 68.4% | 85.5%"]
- result: N2H+ abundance = 7.5e-10 ± within factor of two dimensionless [8 Single-dish N2H+ abundances: "consistent, within a factor of two, with a constant abundance $X(N_2H^+)=7.5\times 10^{-10}$"]
- limitation (authors): HiFIVE fails on the most complex structures (fibers 21, 36/37, 42/43); the 55 fibers are a simplified description [6 Fiber identification: "the above 55 fibers should be interpreted as a simplified description of the real dense gas substructure within the ISF"]
- limitation (authors): Mass conversion uncertain by factor 2-3; lengths not corrected for projection [3.3 Linear masses and stability: "Our length measurements do not consider projection effects making our M$_{\mathrm{lin}}$ values upper limits."]
- not stated (card): Exact HiFIVE code (not released) and implementation details; Line-free channels used for continuum subtraction; clean parameters: cell size, image size, niter, threshold, masks; Flux scale uncertainty and flux density of calibrators; Feathering parameters (single-dish scaling); Rest frequency/hyperfine frequencies used in CLASS HFS fits; Fiber main-axis fitting and length definition details; Whic…

### 2020ApJ...902...99I: ALMA 0"02 Resolution Observations Reveal HCN-abundance-enhanced Counter-rotating and Outflowing Dense Molecular Gas at the NGC 1068 Nucleus (2020; agn_and_quasars, nearby_galaxies, astrochemistry; observational_alma_primary)
- question: Morphological, dynamical, chemical and physical properties of dense molecular gas in the putative dusty molecular torus of the type-2 AGN NGC 1068, probed at ~0.02'' with HCN/HCO+/HNC J=3-2, 13C isotopologue and vibrationally excited lines
- step 3: Make moment 0, 1 and 2 maps and read torus morphology and kinematics
  - method: Intensity-weighted mean velocity (moment 1) maps of HCN and HCO+ J=3-2 shown against moment 0 and moment 2 maps; velocity display range matched to earlier 0.04'' maps [3 Results: "The intensity-weighted mean velocity (moment 1) maps of the HCN J=3–2 and HCO$^{+}$ J=3–2 lines are also displayed in Figure 2 (bottom panels)."]
  - parameter: Continuum peak (~260 GHz) adopted as the SMBH position [3 Results: "We adopt the $\sim$260 GHz continuum peak position as the location of the mass-accreting SMBH"]
  - assumption: V_sys = 1130 km/s, optical LSR velocity [3 Results: "we adopt the systemic velocity of NGC 1068 to be V$_{\rm sys}$ = 1130 km s$^{-1}$ in the optical LSR velocity"]
  - validation: Main Cycle 6 features reproduced in the Cycle 5+6 combined data (Appendix A) [3 Results: "Main features detected in our Cycle 6 data are reproduced in the combined data as well."]
- step 5: Quantify outflow from the C-peak absorption wing
  - method: Correct C-peak spectra for innermost-torus contamination, fit a Gaussian to the broad blueshifted HCN absorption wing, convert equivalent width to HCN J=2 column density with Eq. (3), then to H2 mass and energy [4.7 Outflowing dense molecular gas: "We apply a Gaussian fit to the broad blueshifted HCN J=3–2 absorption wing in the raw beam-sized spectrum at the C-peak in Figure 6a"]
  - parameter: A_ul = 8.4e-4 s^-1 for HCN J=3-2 [4.7 Outflowing dense molecular gas: "where A$_{\rm ul}$ = 8.4 $\times$ 10$^{-4}$ (s$^{-1}$) is adopted for HCN J=3–2 (cite: mul05)"]
  - parameter: HCN spread over the beam, 1.3 pc x 1.2 pc [4.7 Outflowing dense molecular gas: "If these outflowing HCN molecules spatially distribute in the beam size (0.019$''$ $\times$ 0.017$''$ or 1.3 pc $\times$ 1.2 pc; Table 2)"]
  - assumption: HCN-to-H2 abundance ratio of 1e-8 because outflow is confined to the nuclear region [4.7 Outflowing dense molecular gas: "Assuming a high HCN-to-H$_{2}$ abundance ratio of 10$^{-8}$ ($\S$4.1) because the outflow is supposed to be confined to the nuclear region"]
  - assumption: Continuum dust attenuation neglected at ~260 GHz [4.7 Outflowing dense molecular gas: "where continuum flux attenuation by dust extinction is neglected at $\sim$260 GHz ($\sim$1.2 mm)"]
  - fitting: Single Gaussian to the absorption wing in the raw C-peak spectrum [4.7 Outflowing dense molecular gas: "We apply a Gaussian fit to the broad blueshifted HCN J=3–2 absorption wing in the raw beam-sized spectrum at the C-peak in Figure 6a"]
  - validation: Blueshifted HCN wing becomes clearer and an HCO+ wing appears after contamination correction [4.7 Outflowing dense molecular gas: "What is more important is that the blueshifted absorption tail of HCN J=3–2 becomes clearer"]
- step 6: Derive line luminosities and dense molecular gas mass
  - method: Gaussian-fit fluxes in the 14 pc x 7 pc region converted to L and L' with the Solomon & Vanden Bout equations, then J=1-0 luminosity assumed equal to J=3-2 and multiplied by HCN or HCO+ conversion factors [4.2 Estimated torus dense molecular mass: "using the following equations (cite: sol05)"] [4.2 Estimated torus dense molecular mass: "= 6–12 $\times$ HCN J=1–0 luminosity"]
  - parameter: HCO+ conversion: 2–5 x HCO+ J=1-0 luminosity [4.2 Estimated torus dense molecular mass: "= 2–5 $\times$ HCO$^{+}$ J=1–0 luminosity"]
  - assumption: Torus HCN and HCO+ emission almost thermalized up to J=3 and optically thick at J=3-2 and lower, so J=1-0 luminosity equals J=3-2 luminosity [4.2 Estimated torus dense molecular mass: "Assuming that HCN and HCO$^{+}$ emission in the dense and warm NGC 1068 torus is almost thermalized at up to J=3 and optically thick at J=3–2 and lower J-transitions"]
  - assumption: HCO+-derived mass adopted because HCN is overabundant [4.2 Estimated torus dense molecular mass: "By considering the suggested HCN overabundance and the resulting possible overestimatation of HCN-derived H$_{2}$ mass"]
  - validation: Independent size check from assumed 1e6 cm^-3 density and clump filling factor 0.01–0.1 gives 3–6 pc, comparable to the observed size [4.2 Estimated torus dense molecular mass: "This independent, simple comparison also suggests that the estimated torus dense molecular mass is reasonable."]
- step 7: Constrain HCN-to-HCO+ abundance ratio from 13C isotopologue absorption
  - method: Compare H13CN and H13CO+ J=3-2 absorption at the C-peak, correct the ratio for B-coefficient and RADEX excitation differences [4.1 HCN-abundance-enhanced dense molecu…: "We thus use the isotopologue H$^{13}$CN J=3–2 and H$^{13}$CO$^{+}$ J=3–2 line data to constrain the HCN-to-HCO$^{+}$ abundance ratio"]
  - software: RADEX
  - parameter: B23 of H13CO+ ~1.7 times that of H13CN [4.1 HCN-abundance-enhanced dense molecu…: "The B-coefficient from J=2 to J=3 (B$_{\rm 23}$) of H$^{13}$CO$^{+}$ is a factor of $\sim$1.7 higher than that of H$^{13}$CN"]
  - assumption: H13CN/H13CO+ column density ratio equals the HCN/HCO+ abundance ratio; absorption comes from the foreground torus [4.1 HCN-abundance-enhanced dense molecu…: "where we assume that the H$^{13}$CN-to-H$^{13}$CO$^{+}$ column density ratio (= abundance ratio) is comparable to an HCN-to-HCO$^{+}$ abundance ratio."]
  - validation: Consistency with other lines of evidence (flux ratio, spatial extent, mass ratio, VIB lines) [4.1 HCN-abundance-enhanced dense molecu…: "Summarizing, various observational results described in this section consistently suggest an enhanced HCN abundance, relative to HCO$^{+}$, in the NGC 1068 torus."]
- step 9: Characterise vibrationally excited HCN absorption and emission
  - method: Gaussian fit to the HCN-VIB absorption at the C-peak converted to column density via equivalent width; Gaussian fit to HCN-VIB emission at the W-peak converted to luminosity with Eqs. (1)-(2); moment 0 map over 1170–1430 km/s [4.5 Vibrationally excited dense molecul…: "we obtain the absorption equivalent width to be EW $\sim$ 2 $\times$ 10$^{6}$ (cm s$^{-1}$)"] [4.5 Vibrationally excited dense molecul…: "Using Equations (1) and (2), the HCN-VIB J=3–2 emission line luminosity is estimated"]
  - parameter: A_ul = 7.3e-4 s^-1 for HCN-VIB J=3-2 [4.5 Vibrationally excited dense molecul…: "The A$_{ul}$ value for HCN-VIB J=3–2 is 7.3 $\times$ 10$^{-4}$ (s$^{-1}$) at Splatalogue (cite: mul05)."]
  - assumption: HCN-VIB line luminosity from the W-peak taken as the torus total because it is detected clearly only there [4.5 Vibrationally excited dense molecul…: "Since the HCN-VIB J=3–2 emission line is detected clearly only in the beam-sized W-peak spectrum, we use the luminosity as that from the NGC 1068 torus."]
  - fitting: Gaussian fits to the absorption feature and the W-peak emission line [4.5 Vibrationally excited dense molecul…: "A Gaussian fit to this line is shown in Figure 5e."]
  - validation: Moment 0 map of redshifted component detects HCN-VIB at ~4.8 sigma near the W-peak [4.5 Vibrationally excited dense molecul…: "The HCN-VIB J=3–2 emission line is detected with $\sim$4.8$\sigma$ at the northwestern side of the C-peak"]
- result: H13CN J=3-2 moment 0 absorption at the C-peak = -0.090 (~4.5 sigma) ± not stated Jy/beam km/s [4.1 HCN-abundance-enhanced dense molecu…: "a significant ($\sim$4.5$\sigma$) negative absorption signal with $-$0.090 (Jy beam$^{-1}$ km s$^{-1}$) at the C-peak"]
- result: H13CN-to-H13CO+ J=3-2 absorption signal ratio = >1.8 ± not stated dimensionless [4.1 HCN-abundance-enhanced dense molecu…: "the observed H$^{13}$CN-to-H$^{13}$CO$^{+}$ J=3–2 absorption signal ratio with $>$1.8"]
- result: HCN-to-HCO+ abundance ratio in the torus = >3 ± not stated dimensionless [4.1 HCN-abundance-enhanced dense molecu…: "or an HCN-to-HCO$^{+}$ abundance ratio of $>$3, in the NGC 1068 torus"]
- result: HCN-derived dense H2 mass, 14 pc x 7 pc torus region = 2–4e6 ± not stated Msun [4.2 Estimated torus dense molecular mass: "we obtain HCN-derived dense H$_{2}$ mass within the 14 pc $\times$ 7 pc torus region to be M$_{\rm dense-H_2}$(HCN) = 2–4 $\times$ 10$^{6}$M$_{\odot}$"]
- result: HCO+-derived dense H2 mass (adopted torus mass) = 0.4–1.0e6 ± not stated Msun [4.2 Estimated torus dense molecular mass: "M$_{\rm dense-H_2}$(HCO$^{+}$) = 0.4–1.0 $\times$ 10$^{6}$M$_{\odot}$"]
- result: HCN-derived to HCO+-derived dense H2 mass ratio = ~4–5 ± not stated dimensionless [4.2 Estimated torus dense molecular mass: "The dense H$_{2}$ mass derived from HCN is a factor of $\sim$4–5 higher than the one derived from HCO$^{+}$"]
