### 2021A&A...648A..38G: CO<SUP>+</SUP> as a probe of the origin of CO in diffuse interstellar clouds (2021; milky_way_ism, astrochemistry; observational_alma_primary)
- question: Is thermal cosmic-ray and UV-driven chemistry producing CO in diffuse interstellar molecular gas through the intermediate formation of CO+?
- step 2: Integrate opacity and derive column densities / limits
  - method: Integrate line profiles over fixed velocity intervals; 3-sigma upper limits for non-detections; convert integrated opacity to column density [Table 2: "Upper limits are 3$\sigma$."] [3.1 The sight-lines toward J1717-3342 a…: "Therefore, the CO$^+$ column densities have been derived assuming an excitation temperature of 2.73 K."]
  - parameter: Integration velocity intervals -14..10 km/s (J1717-3342) and -20..20 km/s (J1744-3116) [Table 2: "J1717-3342 | 352.7333 | 2.3911 | -14..10"] [Table 2: "J1744-3116 | 357.8635 | -0.9968 | -20..20"]
  - parameter: N(HCO+) = 1.12e12 cm^-2 x integral tau dv [2.4 Other data discussed here: "using N(HCO$^+$) = $1.12\times 10^{12}~{\rm cm}^{-2}\int \tau ~{\rm dv}$ as before"]
  - assumption: CO+ excitation set by the CMB at 2.73 K [2.1 Spectroscopy: "assuming that the energy level population is determined by the cosmic microwave background at 2.73 K"]
  - assumption: 13CO and C18O excitation temperature 5 K [3.1 The sight-lines toward J1717-3342 a…: "we assume an excitation temperature of 5 K (Table 1)"]
- step 3: Derive CO+/HCO+ ratio and X(CO+) limits
  - method: Ratio of N(CO+) limits to N(HCO+) and to N(H2) = N(HCO+)/3e-9 [Table 3: "$^a$N($\mathrm{{\rm H}_2}$) = N(HCO$^+$)/$3\times 10^{-9}$."]
  - assumption: Standard HCO+ abundance relative to H2 of 3e-9 in diffuse molecular gas [3.1 The sight-lines toward J1717-3342 a…: "using the standard value for the HCO$^+$ abundance relative to H$_2$ in the diffuse molecular gas, $3\times 10^{-9}$ (cite: lucas:96; gerin:19)"]
- step 4: Test CO+ + H2 as the HCO+ source (analytic balance)
  - method: Equate HCO+ formation via CO+ + H2 with HCO+ recombination with electrons and solve for required n(CO+)/n(HCO+) and X(CO+); compare with observed limits [3.2 CO+ as a source of HCO+: "Equating formation and recombination of HCO$^+$ , neglecting photodissociation that is four orders of magnitude slower according to the Kinetic Database for Astrochemistry (KIDA)"]
  - software: KIDA
  - parameter: x(e) = 2e-4 from n(C+)/n(H) = 1.6e-4; f_H2 = 0.6, T = 40 K [3.2 CO+ as a source of HCO+: "Taking $x(e)= 2\times10^{-4}$ corresponding to the observed free gas-phase carbon abundance $n({\rm C}^+)/n({\rm H}) = 1.6\times10^{-4}$ (cite: SofLau+04)"] [3.2 CO+ as a source of HCO+: "This is some 25 times higher than our upper limit"]
  - assumption: Steady state; photodissociation neglected; HCO+ destroyed by thermal-electron recombination [3.2 CO+ as a source of HCO+: "neglecting photodissociation that is four orders of magnitude slower according to the Kinetic Database for Astrochemistry (KIDA)"]
- step 5: Model the coupled CO+/HOC+/HCO+ thermal chemistry
  - method: Self-consistent atomic/molecular, ionization and thermal equilibrium in gas spheres with column density varied and abundances integrated along the central line of sight; test variants of rates and abundances (baseline, HOC+ isomerization r… [4 The thermal chemistry of CO+, HOC+ an…: "In brief we calculated the self-consistent global atomic/molecular, ionization and thermal equilibrium in gas spheres of constant total hydrogen number and column density"] [4 The thermal chemistry of CO+, HOC+ an…: "The hydrogen column density was varied and molecular abundances were integrated along the central line of sight to form the results that are displayed here."]
  - software: KIDA
  - parameter: X(OH) = 1.0 +/- 0.14 x 10^-7 [4 The thermal chemistry of CO+, HOC+ an…: "X(OH) $= 1.0 \pm 0.14 \times10^{-7}$ varies only narrowly"]
  - parameter: X(H2O) = 2.4e-8 [4 The thermal chemistry of CO+, HOC+ an…: "whose fixed relative abundance X($\mathrm{{\rm H}_2}$O) $=2.4\times10^{-8}$ we take from (cite: GerNeu+16)"]
  - parameter: X(CH+) free parameter, default 1e-9 [4 The thermal chemistry of CO+, HOC+ an…: "We take X(CH$^+$) as a free parameter with X(CH$^+$) $= 10^{-9}$ by default in the following discussion."]
  - assumption: Interstellar radiation field (Draine field G0 = 1 elsewhere); HOC+ formation via O + CH3+ ignored as non-thermal [4 The thermal chemistry of CO+, HOC+ an…: "We ignore HOC$^+$ formation by the reaction of O with CH$_3$$^+$ because the formation of CH$_3$$^+$ is an aspect of the non-thermal chemistry."]
  - validation: Compared model CO+ and HOC+ abundances with the observed CO+ upper limits and the observed N(HOC+)/N(HCO+) [4 The thermal chemistry of CO+, HOC+ an…: "The model produces CO$^+$ relative abundances about 2.5 times smaller than our observed upper limits, and about twice as much HOC$^+$ as observed."]
- step 6: Derive n(H)/G0 for CO formation from N(CO)/N(HCO+)
  - method: Balance CO formation by HCO+ dissociative recombination against CO photodissociation for a uniform slab and solve for n(H)/G0 as a function of E(B-V) for the QSO sight-line sample [5 Conditions for the growth of CO: "Balancing the formation of CO through dissociative recombination of HCO$^+$ and destruction of CO by photodissociation can constrain the physical conditions where CO might form in diffuse molecular gas."]
  - parameter: T = 40 K, x(e) = 2e-4 [5 Conditions for the growth of CO: "a uniform temperature of 40K and a uniform electron fraction $x(e) = 2 \times 10^{-4}$."]
  - parameter: A_V = 3.1 E(B-V), E(B-V) from Green et al. 2019, nominal error 0.02 mag [5 Conditions for the growth of CO: "The error bar in E(B-V) is a nominal value 0.02 mag."]
  - assumption: Draine radiation field defined as G0 = 1; photodissociation rate 2.43e-10 s^-1 attenuated as exp(-gamma A_V) with gamma = 3.88; line-of-sight averages replace local quantities [5 Conditions for the growth of CO: "replacing local quantities with line of sight averages"]
  - validation: Compared derived densities with those inferred from HCO+ emission brightness and typical diffuse-gas thermal pressures [5 Conditions for the growth of CO: "are higher by a factor $\sim$ 5 than are inferred from the brightness of HCO$^+$ emission in these directions (cite: Lis20Hotspots)"]
