### 2025ApJ...985..259J: Searching for Compact Obscured Nuclei in Compton-thick Active Galactic Nuclei (2025; agn_and_quasars, nearby_galaxies; observational_alma_primary)
- question: Are Compact Obscured Nuclei (CONs, bright HCN-vib cores) present in hard X-ray-confirmed Compton-thick AGN of GOALS U/LIRGs, and do CT AGN show evolved molecular outflows consistent with a CON-to-CT-AGN evolutionary sequence?
- step 3: Make moment 0 maps and measure HCN and HCO+ (3-2) line fluxes, FWHM and surface brightness at the continuum peak
  - method: Beam-sized aperture spectra at the continuum peak; Gaussian FWHM via specutils; flux over twice the FWHM; luminosity from Solomon and Vanden Bout formula divided by aperture area [3.2 Dense gas tracers: "We measure the fluxes of the HCN (3-2) and HCO$^+$ (3-2) spectral lines at the 1-mm continuum peak by extracting spectra with a beam-sized aperture"] [3.2 Dense gas tracers: "We measure spectral line surface densities $\Sigma_{\text{line}}$ by dividing the line luminosity by the area of the beam-sized aperture"]
  - software: Spectral-Cube, Astropy specutils
  - parameter: Moment 0 integrated over all channels with >3 sigma line emission [3.2 Dense gas tracers: "We generate total intensity (moment 0) maps of the two spectral lines by integrating over all channels with"]
  - parameter: Flux integrated over a velocity range of twice the Gaussian FWHM [3.2 Dense gas tracers: "so the fluxes were extracted over a velocity range with a size of twice the Gaussian FWHM"]
  - parameter: Flux uncertainty from spectrum RMS plus an assumed 10% Band 6 flux uncertainty [Table 4 notes: "and an assumed 10% flux uncertainty for ALMA Band 6"]
  - assumption: Rest frequencies from Splatalogue; D_L and z from Armus et al. 2009 [2.2 New ALMA observations: "These rest frequencies were obtained from the astronomical spectroscopy database, Splatalogue."]
  - fitting: Gaussian fit to the line profile for FWHM [Table 4 notes: "This was measured via a Gaussian fit to the line profile."]
- step 4: Place 3-sigma upper limits on HCN-vib luminosity and surface brightness
  - method: Boxcar line profile of width equal to the HCN (3-2) FWHM on central beam spectrum, converted with the luminosity equation and divided by beam area [3.3 Limits on HCN-vib emission: "we place 3$\sigma$ upper limits on the HCN-vib emission by assuming a boxcar line profile with a width equal to the FWHM of the HCN (3-2) line detection"]
  - parameter: Example NGC 4922N: mean 1-sigma noise 0.25 mJy over FWHM 148 km/s gives <0.11 Jy km/s [3.3 Limits on HCN-vib emission: "the NGC 4922N spectrum has a mean 1$\sigma$ noise level of 0.25 mJy."]
  - assumption: CON criterion Sigma_HCN-vib >= 1 Lsun/pc^2 [3.3 Limits on HCN-vib emission: "fall below the 1 $L_\odot$ pc$^{-2}$ threshold for CONs at $\sim$100 pc-scales"]
  - validation: Searched regions surrounding the continuum peak; HCN-vib also undetected [3.3 Limits on HCN-vib emission: "We also explore the regions surrounding the continuum peak, but HCN-vib emission is undetected."]
- step 5: Search for non-circular motions: moment 1/2 maps and position-velocity diagrams
  - method: Moment 1 and 2 maps masked at moment 0 >= 3 sigma; HCN (3-2) PV slices along the major axis through the continuum peak [3.4.1 Nuclear kinematics: "We first analyze the nuclear kinematics of the four observed CT AGN and search for non-circular motions."] [3.4.1 Nuclear kinematics: "PV slices were centered on the 1-mm continuum peak"]
  - software: Spectral-Cube
  - parameter: PV slice width about one beam [3.4.1 Nuclear kinematics: "an approximately beam-sized slice width"]
  - parameter: Systemic velocity from the peak of the HCN (3-2) line in extracted spectra [3.4.1 Nuclear kinematics: "Dashed red lines denote the systemic velocity determined from the peak of the HCN (3-2) line in the extracted spectra"]
- step 6: Map HCN/HCO+ (3-2) luminosity ratio and extract spectra at HCN-enhanced regions for outflow wings
  - method: Ratio of HCN and HCO+ moment 0 maps masked where HCN is below 3 sigma; beam-sized spectra at elevated-ratio positions [3.4.2 HCN/HCO+ line ratio: "We create this Figure by taking the ratio of the HCN (3-2) and HCO$^{+}$ (3-2) total intensity maps after masking out the regions where HCN"]
  - software: Spectral-Cube
  - parameter: NGC 6921 omitted (HCO+ unresolved, low S/N) [3.4.2 HCN/HCO+ line ratio: "NGC 6921 is again omitted from this analysis because the HCO$^+$ (3-2) emission was not spatially resolved and had a low signal-to-noise."]
  - assumption: Elevated HCN/HCO+ can be driven by mechanical heating from outflows [3.4.2 HCN/HCO+ line ratio: "which can be driven by mechanical heating from outflows"]
- step 7: Compare CT AGN HCN-vib and 1-mm surface brightness with CONs and derive CON detection rate
  - method: Re-measure HCN-vib upper limits with continuum-sized apertures, plot against S_1mm/Omega with literature CONs and U/LIRGs, compute detection-rate interval [4.1 Are CT AGN also CONs?: "we compare our complete sample of nine CT AGN to other local U/LIRGs (including CONs) by plotting"] [4.1 Are CT AGN also CONs?: "These values were calculated using spectra extracted with a continuum-sized aperture"]
  - parameter: 1-sigma confidence interval by beta distribution quantile technique [4.1 Are CT AGN also CONs?: "the 1-$\sigma$ confidence intervals were estimated using the beta distribution quantile technique"]
  - assumption: Both continuum peaks of NGC 7130 plotted since the AGN position is unknown (10 points for 9 AGN) [4.1 Are CT AGN also CONs?: "resulting in 10 plotted values for our complete sample of nine CT AGN"]
  - fitting: Binomial population with beta-distribution quantiles [4.1 Are CT AGN also CONs?: "which assigns a probability density function to an assumed binomial population"]
  - validation: Also tested the L_HCN-vib/L_IR > 1e-8 criterion [4.1.1 Impact of Spatial Resolution: "We find that the CT AGN are not CONs with respect to both the"]
- step 8: Test the effect of spatial resolution on the HCN-vib non-detections
  - method: Treat the HCN-vib emission as a point source with fixed luminosity (3-sigma or 1-sigma limit) and solve for the aperture radius at which surface brightness reaches 1 Lsun/pc^2 [4.1.1 Impact of Spatial Resolution: "approximating the HCN-vib emission region as a single-point source for each CT AGN."]
  - assumption: Line luminosity constant; only aperture radius changes [4.1.1 Impact of Spatial Resolution: "this approximation assumes that the line luminosity remains constant, and only the radius of the aperture is adjusted"]
  - validation: Radii below 5 pc are inconsistent with the CON criterion definition [4.1.1 Impact of Spatial Resolution: "smaller radii are inconsistent with the observed properties of CONs."]
- step 9: Assess outflow detectability and census literature outflows in the nine CT AGN
  - method: Scale line luminosities of known CON HCN outflows to the survey sensitivity; tally literature molecular outflows and ages [4.2 Evolutionary link: "we confirm that our sensitivities are sufficient to detect similar outflows in our observed CT sample, if they are present."] [4.2 Evolutionary link: "Of the nine hard X-ray-confirmed CT AGN, we find that at least 44% (4/9) host molecular outflows."]
  - assumption: HCN (3-2)/HCN (1-0) luminosity ratio of 0.5 [4.2 Evolutionary link: "The latter calculation assumes an HCN (3-2) to HCN (1-0) luminosity ratio of 0.5 (cite: Aalto2015b; Gorski2024)."]
