### 2022RAA....22f5021G: Phenol in High-mass Star-forming Regions (2022; astrochemistry, star_formation, milky_way_ism; theory_or_simulation_compared_to_alma)
- question: What are the thermodynamic properties of the C6H6O isomeric group, how is phenol formed in high-mass star-forming regions and dark clouds, and what is the observational upper limit on phenol abundance in the hot molecular core G10.47+0.03?
- step 3: Model the astrochemical evolution of phenol in hot cores and dark clouds
  - method: Run a 3-stage gas-grain chemical network (isothermal collapse, warm-up, post-warm-up) for a hot core (models C1-C10) and a static model for a starless dark cloud (model DC), testing combinations of gas-phase and ice-phase formation pathway… [4. Astrochemical Model: "Here, Chemical Model of Molecular Cloud is implemented (Das et al. 2019, 2021; Gorai et al. 2020; Mondal et al. 2021; Sil et al. 2021; Bhat et al. 2022) to study the chemical evolution of phenol in high-mass star-forming regions and dark cloud environments."] [4.1. Hot Core Model: "We prepare various models to study the fate of the phenol production in such environments. Models are prepared based on the reactions (1), (3), (4), and (5) (see Table 1)."]
  - software: Chemical Model of Molecular Cloud
  - parameter: Hot core: n_H0 = 3 x 10^3 cm^-3 to n_Hmax = 2 x 10^8 cm^-3 (collapse, 10^5 yr, T_gas = 10 K, A_V = 2 to A_V0*(n_H/n_H0)^(2/3)), warm-up to 200 K in 10^5 yr then to 400 K in 2.12 x 10^4 yr, post-warm-up for 10^5 yr (total 3.212 x 10^5 yr) [4.1. Hot Core Model: "Here, following Garrod et al. (2017), for the isothermal collapse phase, we consider minimum density (n H0 ) = 3 × 10 [3] cm [−][3] and maximum density ( n Hmax ) = 2 ´ 10 8 cm [−][3] ."] [4.1. Hot Core Model: "During the initial warm-up phase, the cloud is allowed to warm up (gas and grain both) to 200 K in 10 [5] yr. Then another 2.12 × 10 [4] yr is used to warm up to 400 K. Finally, another 10 [5] yr is used for the post-warm-up phase, where temperature, density, and visual extinction are kept constant at their respective…"]
  - parameter: Dark cloud (DC): n_H = 2.0 x 10^4 cm^-3, T_gas = T_ice = 20 K, A_V = 10, time = 1.0 x 10^7 yr [4.2. Dark Cloud Model: "For the dark cloud model DC, some standard parameters are implemented (n H = 2.0 × 10 [4] cm [−][3], T gas = T ice = 20 K, A V = 10, time = 1.0 × 10 [7] yr)."]
  - parameter: Binding energies: 2056 K for C6H5 and 5476 K for C6H5OH (scaled by 1.416) [4.1. Hot Core Model: "As a result, we obtain binding energy (scaled by 1.416, because we use water monomer as a substrate here) of C 6 H 5 and C 6 H 5 OH as 2056 K and 5476 K, respectively."]
  - assumption: Destruction of phenol in gas phase via photodissociation (alpha = 2.9 x 10^-9, beta = 0, gamma = 1.7), cosmic ray dissociation (alpha = 1.3 x 10^-17, beta = 0, gamma = 263.5), and major ions (C+, He+, HCO+, H3O+) [4.1. Hot Core Model: "cosmic ray dissociation (α = 1.3 × 10 [−][17], β = 0, and γ = 263.5) are considered. Furthermore, its destruction by the major ions (C [+], He [+], HCO [+], and H 3 O [+] ) is also considered."]
  - validation: Compare peak modeled gas-phase abundances across models C1-C10 against the observational upper limit of 5.19 x 10^-9 in G10.47+0.03. [5. Conclusions: "All these obtained peak abundances are consistent (below) with the upper limit (5.19 × 10 [−][9] ) set by the data analysis of a Galactic high-mass star-forming region, G10."]
- result: Peak modeled gas-phase phenol abundance in hot core (Model C8, upper limit) = 2.20e-9 ± not stated dimensionless [Abstract: "Our astrochemical model delivers an upper limit on phenol abundance of ∼2.20 × 10 [−][9] in the hot molecular core, whereas its production in the dark cloud is not satisfactory."]
- result: Peak modeled gas-phase phenol abundance in hot core (Model C9) = 1.09e-11 ± not stated dimensionless [4.1. Hot Core Model: "With the consideration of the lower leading rate constant, phenol abundance drops to 1.09 × 10 [−][11] ."]
- result: Peak modeled gas-phase phenol abundance in hot core (Models C1, C4-C7, C10) = 1.67e-12 ± not stated dimensionless [4.1. Hot Core Model: "The peak abundance obtained with this model is 1.67 × 10 [−][12] for this."]
