### 2018ApJ...856..143I: ALMA Multiple-transition Observations of High-density Molecular Tracers in Ultraluminous Infrared Galaxies (2018; nearby_galaxies, agn_and_quasars, astrochemistry; observational_alma_primary)
- question: How do HCN, HCO+ and HNC line flux ratios vary between J=4-3 and J=3-2 in ULIRGs, and do AGN-containing ULIRGs show higher HCN-to-HCO+ ratios than starbursts, because of abundance or excitation?
- step 4: Compute line flux ratios (J=4-3/J=3-2, HCN/HCO+, HCN/HNC)
  - method: Ratios of Gaussian-fit fluxes in Jy km/s, with J=3-2 HCN and HCO+ from earlier paper [4. RESULTS: "Table 11 tabulates the ratios of J=4–3 to J=3–2 flux in (Jy km s [−][1] ) for HCN, HCO [+], and HNC."]
  - assumption: ~10% absolute flux calibration uncertainty added to HCN/HNC ratios [Table 12: "possible ∼10% absolute flux calibration uncertainty is added."]
- step 5: Estimate line opacity attenuation from isotopologue ratios
  - method: Compare main-to-isotopologue flux ratios against 12C/13C abundance ratio of 50-100; derive attenuation ratio between HCN and HCO+/HNC and correct intrinsic ratios [5.2.3. Intrinsic HCN-to-HCO [+] and HCN…: "if the observed flux ratios are smaller than the 12 C-to- 13 C abundance ratio of 50–100 estimated for ULIRGs"]
  - assumption: Isotopologue lines optically thin; correction independent of absolute 12C/13C and alpha [5.2.3. Intrinsic HCN-to-HCO [+] and HCN…: "is independent of the adopted absolute value of the 12 C-to- 13 C abundance ratio (between 50–100) and the α value"]
- step 6: Constrain gas density and temperature with RADEX
  - method: LVG RADEX grids of J=4-3/J=3-2 ratios vs Tkin and nH2, compared with observed ratios [5.2.4. RADEX Calculations and Molecular…: "using the large velocity gradient (LVG) method in the widely used RADEX code (van der Tak et al. 2007)"]
  - software: RADEX
  - parameter: line widths 400 and 1000 km/s [5.2.4. RADEX Calculations and Molecular…: "line widths of 400 km s [−][1] and 1000 km s [−][1] are used to represent all galaxies (except for Superantennae) and Superantennae, respectively."]
  - assumption: Column density of 1e16 cm-2 for HCN, HCO+ and HNC; CMB 3 K included [5.2.4. RADEX Calculations and Molecular…: "We thus assume that the column densities of HCN, HCO [+], and HNC are 1 × 10 [16] cm [−][2]"]
- step 7: Derive outflow column density, mass and rate for IRAS 15250+3609
  - method: Absorption equivalent widths converted to column density; outflow mass from redshifted emission sub-peak luminosity; rate = 3 x Mout x V/R (or without 3) [5.1.1. IRAS 15250+3609: "Adopting the outflowing gas size of R ∼ 80 pc from the nucleus and the relation of M˙ outf = 3 × M outf × V/R (Maiolino et al. 2012; Cicone et al. 2014)"]
  - parameter: V = 500 km/s, R ~ 80 pc [5.1.1. IRAS 15250+3609: "the outflow velocity of V = 500 km s [−][1] is adopted for IRAS 15250+3609."]
  - assumption: HCO+-to-H2 abundance ~1e-8; Mdense = 10 x HCN J=1-0 luminosity; lines thermalized and optically thick [5.1.1. IRAS 15250+3609: "Adopting the HCO [+] -to-H 2 abundance ratio of ∼10 [−][8] (Greve et al. 2009)"]
  - validation: Mout from J=4-3 and J=3-2 agree within ~30% [5.1.1. IRAS 15250+3609: "Both M outf values agree within ∼30%."]
- result: HCN/HCO+ flux ratio at J=4-3, IRAS 15250+3609 = 3.0 ± ±0.7 dimensionless [Table 12: "IRAS 15250+3609 3.0±0.7 2.6±0.4 0.7±0.1 0.8±0.1"]
- result: HCN/HCO+ flux ratio at J=4-3, NGC 1614 = 0.18 ± ±0.06 dimensionless [Table 12: "NGC 1614 [n] 0.18±0.06 0.33±0.11 1.6±0.7 2.2±1.1"]
