### 2025A&A...693A.199C: Interaction between the supernova remnant W44 and the infrared dark cloud G034.77-00.55: Shock induced star formation (2025; star_formation, milky_way_ism, astrochemistry; archival_reuse)
- question: Does the W44-driven shock induce early stage star formation in G034.77-00.55?
- step 2: Fit molecular columns and excitation temperatures
  - method: Fit LTE models simultaneously to J=1-0 and J=3-2 spectra for each species [D/H at the W44-G034.77 shock interface:…: "we consider the species excitation temperatures as free parameters and use madcuba to obtain the best LTE models that simultaneously reproduce the J=1-0 and J=3-2 transitions."]
  - software: madcuba, SLIM, AUTOFIT
  - parameter: APEX nFLASH240; tuning frequencies 231.320 and 279.512 GHz; frequency resolution 488 kHz [APEX and IRAM-30m observations: "The nFLASH240 receiver was used with tuning frequencies 231.320 and 279.512 GHz and frequency resolution 488 kHz."]
  - parameter: N2D+(3-2): 0.7 km s^-1, 27 arcsec; N2H+(3-2): 0.5 km s^-1, 23 arcsec [APEX and IRAM-30m observations: "the velocity resolutions and beam sizes are 0.7 km s$^{-1}$ and 27$^{\prime\prime}$ for N$_2$D$^+$(3-2), 0.5 km s$^{-1}$ and 23$^{\prime\prime}$ for N$_2$H$^+$(3-2)."]
  - parameter: Convert T*_A to T_mb with beam efficiency 0.77 and forward efficiency 0.95 [APEX and IRAM-30m observations: "Observations were obtained in unit of antenna temperature, T$^*_A$, and converted to main beam temperature T$_{\rm{mb}}$ using beam and forward efficiency of 0.77 and 0.95, respectively."]
  - parameter: APEX rms: 3 mK for N2D+(3-2), 7 mK for N2H+(3-2) [APEX and IRAM-30m observations: "The achieved rms are 3 mK for N$_2$D$^+$(3-2) and 7 mK for N$_2$H$^+$(3-2)"]
  - assumption: LTE; beam filling factor 1 for all transitions [D/H at the W44-G034.77 shock interface:…: "We set the angular size of the emission, $\theta_S$, to reflect the assumption that the emission fills the beam i.e., filling factor of 1."] [D/H at the W44-G034.77 shock interface:…: "We thus maintain the assumption of emission filling the beam for all transitions."]
  - assumption: Shared velocity, FWHM, angular size and excitation temperature across transitions fitted simultaneously [D/H at the W44-G034.77 shock interface:…: "AUTOFIT assumes that $v$, FWHM, $\theta_S$, and T$_{\rm{ex}}$ are the same for all transitions that are fitted simultaneously."]
  - fitting: SLIM AUTOFIT; parameters N_tot, radial systemic velocity, FWHM, T_ex and angular size; T_ex free [D/H at the W44-G034.77 shock interface:…: "Hence, we used the built-in AUTOFIT function of SLIM to obtain the LTE models that best reproduce all transitions."] [D/H at the W44-G034.77 shock interface:…: "assuming as input parameters the total molecular column density (N$_{\rm{tot}}$), the radial systemic velocity of the source ($v$), the line full width at half maximum (FWHM), the excitation temperature (T$_{\rm{ex}}$), and the angular size of the emission ($\theta_S$)."]
  - validation: Models compared with spectra; T_ex errors <10%; velocity and linewidth errors combined with channel width; fitted optical depths <<1 [D/H at the W44-G034.77 shock interface:…: "The uncertainties given by madcuba are $<$10% for T$_{\rm{ex}}$ and $\sim$2% for $v_{\rm{0}}$ and $\Delta v$."] [D/H at the W44-G034.77 shock interface:…: "The final uncertainty on the last two quantities is obtained by summing in quadrature the madcuba error with the spectra channel width (0.6-0.8 km s$^{-1}$ or $\sim$10%)."]
- step 3: Derive deuterium fractionation
  - method: D_frac(N2H+)=N(N2D+)/N(N2H+) [D/H at the W44-G034.77 shock interface:…: "D_{\rm frac}^{\rm N_2H^+} = \frac{N({\rm N_2D^+})}{N({\rm N_2H^+})},"]
  - software: madcuba
  - fitting: Column densities from the multi-transition LTE fits [D/H at the W44-G034.77 shock interface:…: "we consider the species excitation temperatures as free parameters and use madcuba to obtain the best LTE models that simultaneously reproduce the J=1-0 and J=3-2 transitions."]
  - validation: Comparison with previous D/H analysis; Ambient upper limit insensitive to changing T_ex to 4.5 K [D/H at the W44-G034.77 shock interface:…: "The 3$\sigma$ D/H upper limit previously estimated for the Ambient region does not change significantly when T$_{\rm{ex}}$=4.5 K is assumed."] [D/H at the W44-G034.77 shock interface:…: "The multi-transition approach here adopted confirms the results reported in (cite: cosentino2023) and its implications."]
- step 4: Map dense-gas morphology and velocity
  - method: Construct integrated intensity and moment 1 maps using the isolated hyperfine component [N$_2$H$^+$ kinematics and morphology: f…: "The N$_2$H$^+$ integrated intensity map (39-45.5 km s$^{-1}$; top panel) is shown"] [N$_2$H$^+$ kinematics and morphology: f…: "together with the velocity field (moment 1) map (bottom panel)."]
  - parameter: Initial moment-map interval 39-45.5 km s^-1; mask below 3×A_rms [N$_2$H$^+$ kinematics and morphology: f…: "The N$_2$H$^+$ integrated intensity map (39-45.5 km s$^{-1}$; top panel)"] [N$_2$H$^+$ kinematics and morphology: f…: "the pixels below 3$\times$A$_{rms}$"]
  - assumption: Isolated N2H+ hyperfine component is optically thin [N$_2$H$^+$ kinematics and morphology: f…: "the isolated component of the N$_2$H$^+$ hyper-fine structure, which is optically thin."]
- step 5: Separate velocity components
  - method: Multi-Gaussian fitting of ~30000 spectra; build velocity and FWHM distributions [N$_2$H$^+$ kinematics and morphology: f…: "we have used the semi-automated python tool scousepy (cite: henshaw2016) to perform multi-Gaussian fitting of all the spectra in the data cube."] [N$_2$H$^+$ kinematics and morphology: f…: "We have obtained this information for the N$_2$H$^+$ emission mapped with ALMA ($\sim$30000 spectra fitted)"]
  - software: scousepy
  - parameter: 0.2 km s^-1 channels; isolated N2H+(1-0) hyperfine component [N$_2$H$^+$ kinematics and morphology: f…: "we have re-binned the data to have velocity channel 0.2 km s$^{-1}$ and have considered only the isolated component of the N$_2$H$^+$(1-0) hyper-fine structure."]
  - fitting: Semi-automated multi-Gaussian fits returning velocity, intensity and FWHM [N$_2$H$^+$ kinematics and morphology: f…: "The scousepy output provides information on the velocity, intensity and FWHM of the multiple velocity components toward each positional element in a data cube."]
- step 10: Test thermal and turbulent support
  - method: Calculate α_vir=a×5σ_tot²r/(GM) and classify cores against α_vir=2 [Core stability against thermal, turbule…: "\alpha_{\rm{vir}}=a\frac{5 \sigma_{\rm{tot}}^2 r}{GM}"] [Core stability against thermal, turbule…: "Hence, cores with $\alpha_{\rm{vir}}<$2 are considered bound and unstable against collapse, while cores with $\alpha_{\rm{vir}}>$2 will likely be dispersed."]
  - parameter: σ_tot includes thermal and non-thermal N2H+ broadening [Core stability against thermal, turbule…: "where $\sigma_{\rm{tot}}$ is the N$_2$H$^+$ velocity dispersion including both thermal and non-thermal broadening ((cite: fuller1992); see also Eq. 6 in (cite: barnes2023))."]
  - assumption: a=2±1; magnetic fields omitted; infalling and outflowing material omitted [Core stability against thermal, turbule…: "In the idealised case of a spherical core of uniform density supported by only kinetic energy (i.e. neglecting magnetic fields)"] [Core stability against thermal, turbule…: "does not take into account contributions due to in-falling and/or out-flowing material."]
  - validation: ~40% α_vir uncertainty dominated by mass surface density; compare trend with barnes2021 [Core stability against thermal, turbule…: "However, the uncertainty on $\alpha_{\rm{vir}}$ accounts for $\sim$40$\%$, with the major contribution due to the mass surface density uncertainty."] [Core stability against thermal, turbule…: "we also compare the relation between virial parameter and core masses from this work with that inferred by (cite: barnes2021)."]
- result: Shock D_frac(N2H+), This work = 0.080 ± ±0.006 dimensionless [Table 2: "Shock"]
