- step 4: Train the HCN/HNC emulator
  - method: Train one fully connected TensorFlow network to predict log HCN and HNC fractional abundances from four physical inputs. [Neural Network Architecture: "We use the python package `TensorFlow`"] [Neural Network Architecture: "We start with an input layer of four nodes, where each node corresponds to one of our chemical model parameters"]
  - software: TensorFlow
  - parameter: 4 layers of 2000 nodes; 12,020,002 trainable parameters [Neural Network Architecture: "we found a neural network with 4 layers each containing 2000 nodes produced results that aligned best with chemical model calculations."] [Neural Network Architecture: "a neural network with fully connected layers and 12,020,002 trainable parameters"]
  - parameter: ReLU activation [Neural Network Architecture: "Activation functions, such as the rectified linear unit (ReLU) that we employ, allow neural networks to consider non-linear solutions."]
  - parameter: Log inputs for n_H2, ζ, and N_H2 [Training Dataset: "using the log of the parameter value for $n_{\text{H}_2}$, $\zeta$, and $N_{\text{H}_2}$"]
  - parameter: Reported split: training 60% (∼69,000), validation 50% (∼17,000), test 25% (∼28,000) [Training Dataset: "a training set (60% of data, $\sim$69,000 points), validation set (50% of data, $\sim$17,000 points), and a test set (25% of data, $\sim$28,000 points)."]
  - parameter: Validation mini-batch size 64 [Neural Network Architecture: "We calculate the validation loss using mini batches with a batch size of 64."]
  - parameter: Train with density buffer points; exclude buffer ranges from Bayesian inference. [Training Results: "we include additional column and volume density buffer points outside of our desired parameter ranges"] [Training Results: "though values outside of this original range will not be considered later in our Bayesian analysis."]
  - assumption: Joint HCN/HNC model based on their similar spatial distributions. [Training Dataset: "We train one neural network model to predict the abundance of both HCN and HNC since previous ALCHEMI studies have shown that these molecules have similar spatial distributions (cite: Behrens2022)."]
