### 2023ApJ...942...25F: The State of the Molecular Gas in Post-starburst Galaxies (2023; nearby_galaxies, star_formation; observational_alma_primary)
- question: What physical properties and excitation states of the molecular gas in post-starburst galaxies prevent their large gas reservoirs from collapsing and forming stars?
- step 2: Construct CO SLEDs and model molecular gas excitation with RADEX
  - method: Combine ALMA CO (3-2) fluxes with IRAM 30m CO (1-0) and CO (2-1) measurements; model line ratios (including HCN and HCO+ individually) on a logarithmic grid of temperature, density, and CO column density using non-LTE code RADEX; compute p… [Gas Excitation: "We combine the CO (3–2) measurements of three post-starburst galaxies from this survey with CO (2–1) and CO (1–0) measurements from the IRAM 30m from (cite: French2015)."] [Gas Excitation: "We explore these possibilities more quantitatively by modelling the CO SLEDs with RADEX (cite: radex)."]
  - software: RADEX
  - parameter: Temperature grid T = 10-300 K; density grid n = 10^1-10^7 cm^-3; CO column density grid N = 10^13-10^21 cm^-2 [Gas Excitation: "We use a grid of logarithmically-spaced values in temperature ($T$; $10-300$ K), density ($n$; $10^1-10^7$ cm$^{-3}$), and column density of CO ($N$; $10^{13}-10^{21}$ cm$^{-2}$), similar to that used by (cite: Krips2011)."]
  - assumption: Uniform sphere geometry, single temperature component [Gas Excitation: "Here, we assume a uniform sphere geometry"] [Gas Excitation: "In our RADEX analysis, we have assumed the molecular gas is composed of a single temperature component."]
  - assumption: Abundance ratio [HCO+/CO] = 10^-4 and [HCN/CO] = 10^-4 [Gas Excitation: "For both dense gas tracers, we assume an abundance ratio with respect to CO of $10^{-4}$ (cite: Krips2008; Aalto2012)"]
  - assumption: ~37% uncertainty from combining ALMA and IRAM 30m measurements assumed for S02 based on H02 and H03 CO (2-1) comparison [Gas Excitation: "For H02 and H03, the ALMA and IRAM CO (2–1) measurements differ by $\sim37$% (consistent within 1.5$\sigma$ of the combined uncertainty)."] [Gas Excitation: "We do not have ALMA CO (2–1) measurements for S02, so we assume it will have a similar uncertainty."]
  - fitting: Grid likelihood evaluation over temperature, density, and column density [Gas Excitation: "We visualize these degeneracies by plotting the likelihoods for the temperature, density, and column density of each source in Figure 2."]
  - validation: Compared ALMA CO (2-1) from Smercina et al. (2022) to IRAM 30m CO (2-1) from French et al. (2015) to verify single dish and interferometric consistency. [Gas Excitation: "We compare the ALMA CO(2–1) flux measurements from (cite: Smercina2022) to the IRAM 30m measurements from (cite: French2015) to estimate the uncertainties introduced from combining the measurements."]
- step 5: Analyze time evolution of sSFR, CO gas fraction, and dense gas fraction with post-burst age
  - method: Fit two-component piecewise linear models to post-burst age vs ln(sSFR) and ln(CO gas fraction); evaluate Spearman and Pearson correlation tests for ages > 400 Myr; compare single-slope and two-slope models using reduced chi^2 and BIC; exa… [Evolution During the Post-Starburst Pha…: "We explore the evolution during the post-starburst phase quantitatively by fitting the sSFR and CO-traced molecular gas fraction trends with two-component linear fits to $x - ln(y)$, with the two lines required to meet in the middle."] [Evolution During the Post-Starburst Pha…: "The two-slope fit is significantly preferred over a single-slope fit using either the reduced $\chi^2$ ($\chi^2/\nu_{1 \ \rm{slope}} = 208$ vs. $\chi^2/\nu_{2 \ \rm{slope}} = 19$) or Bayesian Information Criterion (BIC) ($\rm{BIC}_{1 \ \rm{slope}} =1.5\times10^4$ vs. $\rm{BIC}_{2 \ \rm{slope}} =1326$) tests."]
  - parameter: 4 free parameters: early slope, late slope, break point, y-offset [Evolution During the Post-Starburst Pha…: "This model has four free parameters: the early slope, the late slope, the break point, and a y-offset."]
  - assumption: Constant uncertainty in sSFR of 0.1 dex; no uncertainty on age; alpha_HCO+ = 10 M_sun (K km s^-1 pc^2)^-1 [Evolution During the Post-Starburst Pha…: "We assume a constant uncertainty in sSFR of 0.1 dex and take into account the measured molecular gas fraction uncertainties and upper limits. For this analysis, we do not include the uncertainty on age."] [Evolution During the Post-Starburst Pha…: "If we instead consider the dense gas to stellar mass fraction, using an $\alpha_{HCO^+}=10$ $M_{\ensuremath{\odot}}$ (K km s$^{-1}$ pc$^2$)$^{-1}$,"]
  - fitting: Piecewise linear fit evaluated with reduced chi^2 and Bayesian Information Criterion (BIC) [Evolution During the Post-Starburst Pha…: "The two-slope fit is significantly preferred over a single-slope fit using either the reduced $\chi^2$ ($\chi^2/\nu_{1 \ \rm{slope}} = 208$ vs. $\chi^2/\nu_{2 \ \rm{slope}} = 19$) or Bayesian Information Criterion (BIC) ($\rm{BIC}_{1 \ \rm{slope}} =1.5\times10^4$ vs. $\rm{BIC}_{2 \ \rm{slope}} =1326$) tests."]
  - validation: Spearman and Pearson correlation tests for >400 Myr; model comparison via reduced chi^2 and BIC. [Evolution During the Post-Starburst Pha…: "We see no evidence of a significant correlation between age and sSFR for ages $>400$ Myr using a Spearman or Pearson correlation test."]
- step 6: Compare dense gas luminosities and line ratios (HCO+/CO, HCN/CO, HCN/HCO+, HNC/HCN) and evaluate SFR tracer biases
  - method: Compare L'_CO, L'_HCO+, and L'_HCN vs SFR and line ratios against galaxy comparison samples using Mann-Whitney U test; compare Halpha SFRs against Neon, TIR, 1.4 GHz, and Br-gamma SFRs; fit linear relation to log(SFR-TIR) vs log(SFR-Neon) … [Star Formation in the Dense Gas: "We also compare the HCO$+$/CO and HCN/CO ratios for the post-starbursts and comparison samples using a Mann-Whitney U test (cite: MannWhitney1947)."] [Dense Gas State: "We plot the comparison of the HCN, HCO$+$, and HNC (1–0) ratios in Figure 15."]
